s agree at roughly 89%. Barr's solid fundraising and Democratic organizing in Traverse City and Marquette suggest some effort but nothing indicating a genuine toss-up.", "watch": "Any late polling or a surge of Democratic outside spending in this upper-Michigan district would be the first sign the race is tightening beyond expectations.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Jack Bergman (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Northern Michigan's 1st District is a solidly Republican seat, and incumbent Jack Bergman is seeking another term against Democrat Callie Barr in a rematch. The district's partisan lean favors Republicans by about eleven points, and every major race rater places the contest at Likely R or stronger, with most calling it Solid or Safe Republican. Prediction markets similarly give Bergman roughly a nine-in-ten chance of winning.\n\nBarr has run a serious campaign, raising about $1.7 million and campaigning across the district, and Democrats have held rallies in Traverse City and Marquette. But there are no public polls showing a competitive race, Bergman has out-raised her, and the fundamentals of the seat remain heavily favorable to him. The forecast expects Bergman to win by a double-digit margin, with modest uncertainty given the absence of polling.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0479, "margin": -11.4}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0291, "margin": -14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0616, "margin": -11.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0563, "margin": -12.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0486, "margin": -11.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.0433, "margin": -12.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.0528, "margin": -12.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.0342, "margin": -13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.0842, "margin": -10.5}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.0576, "margin": -11.5}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "